#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596" :question/problem "![](images/ee/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198080 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198083 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" :question/math-academy-id "e-12606" :question/problem "![](images/44/44ef8fc83ea0a70dcc16769ee6965727ec2c9584e97cc417e34184d633bbf876.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-6x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=6x-6 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''(1) =6 > 0.$ Therefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(1)^{2}-6(1)+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4020acf4-04e1-5b60-a2f7-a157b1314764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x{log}_{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330520} {:answer/id #uuid "731eefef-20d5-574f-9043-1000fa8c1ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330987} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4ee1b1-d9b2-51c6-abc3-dc91b813f3ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{log}_{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331379} {:answer/id #uuid "c509c861-23e4-5b8d-8b8d-1044b852afdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331700} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2baf64-fef0-5fb3-8a66-3f57f615f35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c509c861-23e4-5b8d-8b8d-1044b852afdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331700} :answer-field/id #uuid "497ee4c0-825b-5385-b6b4-603172d90ac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0b5476f-0e78-5366-afe4-a0704f4f9240" :question/math-academy-id "q-49051" :question/problem "![](images/22/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
